A||||4||Loki||New car has a problem***NEED HELP ASAP***||||1||
Z||000000||Loki||02-26-2001||12:30 PM||||OK, I just made a move from texas to north dakota..The entire coolant system was flushed and filled 50/50....This morning I let it run for say 20 or so minutes and I noticed from the thermostat housing was steaming bad and wanting to pop off.....Well I killed the engine and let it cool enough to take the cap off....Refilled anti-freeze and water and ran it a bit without stat, thinking it may be bad..Nope I was wrong still doing it...Took cap off agian, didnt wait long enough so who knows where the pressure blew it to, but is it possible it could be a bad cap? or what else should I look at?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/loki137/loki5.jpg">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net4/headshot.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||02-26-2001||02:22 PM||||You probably have air in the system and it is not circulating properly. When you flushed and refilled the system, Did you fill it correctly? Did you "burp" the system?<BR>Park the car with the nose pointing down. Remove cap and thermostat by the engine. Then SLOWLY open the front radiator cap. You should hear air escaping than coolant coming out. Once the coolant is coming out without bubbles, close radiator cap and fill from back.<P>Normally when the system is flushed you just remove the radiator cap and fill from back until coolant overflows. Cap radiator and fill the rest up from the back. It works better when the rear of the car is up a little.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Loki||02-26-2001||03:13 PM||||Yeah it was filled "correctly" but this is odd, I drove it for like 3 days playing around in Texas and then made the move to ND...It ran fine saturday when we got here..Just let it run for a bit today and it started this....Im gonna try a new cap first and maybe, just maybe the cold weather change affected the seal on the cap..Thanx for the info....<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/loki137/loki5.jpg">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net4/headshot.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Loki||02-26-2001||03:48 PM||||Well so far it seems it was just a bad cap.....Or just didnt adjust to the cold   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  anywho....Least its working now<P><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/loki137/loki5.jpg">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net4/headshot.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||02-26-2001||03:51 PM||||Sounds like you're right about the bad cap. The thermostat cap shouldn't vent. It should vent to the overflow by the radiator.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
